(1078513, 129014, 4411, '<td valign="top">Didn''t get time to comment on this during the challenge. Was chuffed when I saw it in there though : )<br/> <br/>Ed has nailed the difference between this and mine in his comment ... the blue tube light. I owe 2 of my ribbons to that baby!</td>', 'Didn''t get time to comment on this during the challenge. Was chuffed when I saw it in there though : ) Ed has nailed the difference between this and mine in his comment ... the blue tube light. I owe 2 of my ribbons to that baby!', '2004-12-27 03:23:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078591, 129156, 23456, '<td valign="top">Thankyou everyone for your kind words and comments! I think if this was in a different challenge it may have placed higher for the average score received (6.2), normally that would be around the top 10.....oh well I''ll keep at it ! I am more than pleased with this average score  so early on in my DPC submissions ! <br/></td>', 'Thankyou everyone for your kind words and comments! I think if this was in a different challenge it may have placed higher for the average score received (6.2), normally that would be around the top 10.....oh well I''ll keep at it ! I am more than pleased with this average score  so early on in my DPC submissions ! ', '2004-12-27 06:18:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078594, 129214, 8643, '<td valign="top">Deja Vu was all about past ribbon winners - so my grateful thanks to Jacko and Rackat who provided the inspiration and creativity.  All credit to them.<br/>Thank you voters and especially you commentators for such kindness, you have been a geart motivating force throughout the week!<br/>I will write a "how did they do that" shortly because I learned so much from this exercise, some of which it may be worth sharing.<br/>For the moment: it took 4 sessions to reach my goal, spread over two days - the bisected splash was there by design, not chance!<br/>My daughter-in-law went into labour between sessions 2 and 3 and gave birth to our first grandchild during session 4.  As she was 3,500 miles away in MD USA, I needed something to take my mind off the "what ifs" that were crowding in.<br/>Ellie Grace Letheren was born at 9.26 am EST on Sunday 19 January - pictures <a href="https://really-useless.com/gallery/categories.php?cat_id=43" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">here</a>.<br/>How I succeeded with the PS bit and the actual submission, after the news had broken, I know not!</td>', 'Deja Vu was all about past ribbon winners - so my grateful thanks to Jacko and Rackat who provided the inspiration and creativity.  All credit to them.Thank you voters and especially you commentators for such kindness, you have been a geart motivating force throughout the week!I will write a "how did they do that" shortly because I learned so much from this exercise, some of which it may be worth sharing.For the moment: it took 4 sessions to reach my goal, spread over two days - the bisected splash was there by design, not chance!My daughter-in-law went into labour between sessions 2 and 3 and gave birth to our first grandchild during session 4.  As she was 3,500 miles away in MD USA, I needed something to take my mind off the "what ifs" that were crowding in.Ellie Grace Letheren was born at 9.26 am EST on Sunday 19 January - pictures here.How I succeeded with the PS bit and the actual submission, after the news had broken, I know not!', '2004-12-27 06:48:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078596, 129478, 23458, '<td valign="top">Well, would you look at that, a ribbon at last - for some reason I had a hunch this was yours: I think because of the reference you made to trying to do the multi-image comp in the lead up to voting. Well that, and that distinctive border of yours again :P.<br/> <br/>Anyway, what impresses me (and more than a few others by the looks of it) about this is the way you managed the reflections in the waterdrops while maintaining the flurescent background. Would be great if you could post something to explain. <br/> <br/>Congrats.</td>', 'Well, would you look at that, a ribbon at last - for some reason I had a hunch this was yours: I think because of the reference you made to trying to do the multi-image comp in the lead up to voting. Well that, and that distinctive border of yours again :P. Anyway, what impresses me (and more than a few others by the looks of it) about this is the way you managed the reflections in the waterdrops while maintaining the flurescent background. Would be great if you could post something to explain.  Congrats.', '2004-12-27 06:56:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078601, 129270, 8643, '<td valign="top">This is a superb shot and , no offence to Jacko, is better than the original.  We (you and I) had the opportunity to study his fabulous work and then focus on how to produce a similar image without wondering how it would look - the composition and style had already produced a winner!<br/>The vibrancy of your colours is amazing - I chose a different selection "to be different" and ended up with a more subdued look.<br/>I know only too well how much attention to detail was required to produce such a crisp, bright, square, flawless image - my compliments again!<br/>This deserved a ribbon - sorry to have helped to ease you out.</td>', 'This is a superb shot and , no offence to Jacko, is better than the original.  We (you and I) had the opportunity to study his fabulous work and then focus on how to produce a similar image without wondering how it would look - the composition and style had already produced a winner!The vibrancy of your colours is amazing - I chose a different selection "to be different" and ended up with a more subdued look.I know only too well how much attention to detail was required to produce such a crisp, bright, square, flawless image - my compliments again!This deserved a ribbon - sorry to have helped to ease you out.', '2004-12-27 07:08:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078604, 128954, 27431, '<td valign="top">Hey, Ben...Congrats on the elusive 6.6. LOL</td>', 'Hey, Ben...Congrats on the elusive 6.6. LOL', '2004-12-27 07:09:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078637, 129295, 2716, '<td valign="top">Oops ... proper link to the original:<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=25290" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/107/thumb/25290.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/107/thumb/25290.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/107/thumb/25290.jpg"/></a> <br/></td>', 'Oops ... proper link to the original:  ', '2004-12-27 08:17:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078656, 129271, 7910, '<td valign="top">Hey nice shot!  A wonderful tribute to the original.   ;)<br/>Thanks.</td>', 'Hey nice shot!  A wonderful tribute to the original.   ;)Thanks.', '2004-12-27 09:05:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078662, 128588, 7910, '<td valign="top">Interesting effect.  When I first tried this, the thing I found when lighting reflective objects is that you can''t light them directly.  The key is to light whatever will be reflected in them.  Nice job!</td>', 'Interesting effect.  When I first tried this, the thing I found when lighting reflective objects is that you can''t light them directly.  The key is to light whatever will be reflected in them.  Nice job!', '2004-12-27 09:18:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078667, 128553, 18728, '<td valign="top">Thank you everyone who took time to comment &amp; vote. I shall try to improve as per suggestions. For now, enjoy the poetry part of "visual poetry" :)  Thanks</td>', 'Thank you everyone who took time to comment & vote. I shall try to improve as per suggestions. For now, enjoy the poetry part of "visual poetry" :)  Thanks', '2004-12-27 09:24:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078668, 129478, 27293, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by samtrundle:</b></div><hr/><i> Well, would you look at that, a ribbon at last - for some reason I had a hunch this was yours: I think because of the reference you made to trying to do the multi-image comp in the lead up to voting. Well that, and that distinctive border of yours again :P.<br/> <br/>Anyway, what impresses me (and more than a few others by the looks of it) about this is the way you managed the reflections in the waterdrops while maintaining the flurescent background. Would be great if you could post something to explain. <br/> <br/>Congrats. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Naww! The border is from the original image =) I am never putting one on my challenge pics anymore, ''cause even though it''s a vanilla border, people for soem reason recognize it =)<br/> <br/>The reflection vs color is easy, the original shot didn''t have color, just a print of kiwiness'' frames refracted in the waterdrops and blurred in the background. Colors are made with 10 hue/sat colorize adjustment layers with gradient masks, exposing 20% of the pic with the right color. I tried actually doing it with hi-lighters, and you can do the color that way easily, but they appear in the drops too and they cover up the image in the bg, so couldn''t do it that way.<br/> <br/>Thanks everyone!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-12-27 09:32:39.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by samtrundle: Well, would you look at that, a ribbon at last - for some reason I had a hunch this was yours: I think because of the reference you made to trying to do the multi-image comp in the lead up to voting. Well that, and that distinctive border of yours again :P. Anyway, what impresses me (and more than a few others by the looks of it) about this is the way you managed the reflections in the waterdrops while maintaining the flurescent background. Would be great if you could post something to explain.  Congrats.   Naww! The border is from the original image =) I am never putting one on my challenge pics anymore, ''cause even though it''s a vanilla border, people for soem reason recognize it =) The reflection vs color is easy, the original shot didn''t have color, just a print of kiwiness'' frames refracted in the waterdrops and blurred in the background. Colors are made with 10 hue/sat colorize adjustment layers with gradient masks, exposing 20% of the pic with the right color. I tried actually doing it with hi-lighters, and you can do the color that way easily, but they appear in the drops too and they cover up the image in the bg, so couldn''t do it that way. Thanks everyone!', '2004-12-27 09:26:24.000000', '2004-12-27 09:32:39.000000', 0),
(1078676, 129214, 22734, '<td valign="top">You have managed to replicate and then combine two ideas to pull off a unique image that has impressed us all. Congratulations on your well earned blue. Your entry exceeded all expectations.</td>', 'You have managed to replicate and then combine two ideas to pull off a unique image that has impressed us all. Congratulations on your well earned blue. Your entry exceeded all expectations.', '2004-12-27 09:39:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078678, 129377, 22734, '<td valign="top">Sonda: you have outdone yourself here. Congratulations on your red. Way to go!</td>', 'Sonda: you have outdone yourself here. Congratulations on your red. Way to go!', '2004-12-27 09:40:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(1078679, 128384, 9939, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by xtabintun:</b></div><hr/><i> You should''ve flipped it horizontally. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Why?<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by e301:</b></div><hr/><i>This has the look of a 5x7 or even 4x6 torn photo </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Actually it''s an 8x10.<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by e301:</b></div><hr/><i>You''ve certainly got the colour balance of the top image - that''s an important element to me - but your lower is a bit confused.</i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Not sure how you mean- are you referring to the part behind the print, or the lower half of the print?<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-12-27 09:43:16.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by xtabintun: You should''ve flipped it horizontally.   Why? Originally posted by e301:This has the look of a 5x7 or even 4x6 torn photo   Actually it''s an 8x10. Originally posted by e301:You''ve certainly got the colour balance of the top image - that''s an important element to me - but your lower is a bit confused.  Not sure how you mean- are you referring to the part behind the print, or the lower half of the print?', '2004-12-27 09:41:20.000000', '2004-12-27 09:43:16.000000', 0);
